Here’s a step-by-step walkthrough to set up Azure AD B2C for securing a Web API and calling it via Postman.


---

Step 1: Set Up Azure AD B2C Tenant

If you don't already have an Azure AD B2C tenant:

1. In the Azure Portal, search for Azure AD B2C and click Create a B2C Tenant.


2. Follow the instructions to create a tenant and link it to your Azure subscription.




---

Step 2: Register Applications

a. Register the API Application

This represents your Web API.

1. Navigate to Azure AD B2C > App Registrations and click New Registration.


2. Enter the following:

Name: MyWebAPI.

Supported Account Types: Choose Accounts in any identity provider or organizational directory.

Redirect URI: Leave it blank for now.



3. Click Register.


4. After registration, note the Application (Client) ID.


5. Configure API permissions:

Go to Expose an API and click Set to define the Application ID URI (e.g., https://<tenant_name>.onmicrosoft.com/MyWebAPI).

Click Add a Scope and configure:

Scope Name: api.read.

Admin Consent Display Name: Access Web API.

Admin Consent Description: Allows access to the Web API.

Leave State as Enabled.

Click Add Scope.






---

b. Register the Client Application

This represents Postman or any other client accessing your API.

1. Go to Azure AD B2C > App Registrations and click New Registration.


2. Enter the following:

Name: PostmanClient.

Supported Account Types: Choose Accounts in any identity provider or organizational directory.

Redirect URI: Add http://localhost.



3. Click Register.


4. Note the Application (Client) ID.


5. Configure authentication:

Go to Authentication and:

Enable Public client/native.

Save changes.






---

Step 3: Create a User Flow or Custom Policy

User flows define how users authenticate in Azure AD B2C.

1. Go to Azure AD B2C > User Flows and click New User Flow.


2. Select a flow type:

For testing, select Sign up and sign in (recommended for simplicity).



3. Configure the flow:

Name: B2C_1_SignUpSignIn.

Under Identity providers, ensure Email signup is enabled.



4. Click Create.




---

Step 4: Test the User Flow

1. Navigate to User Flows and click on B2C_1_SignUpSignIn.


2. Click Run User Flow and select the PostmanClient application.


3. Enter the Redirect URI as http://localhost.


4. Click Run. This will take you to the authentication page. Complete sign-up/sign-in to test.




---

Step 5: Configure Postman

To call the API, set up Postman to fetch the token and make the request.

a. Fetch Token in Postman

1. Open Postman and create a new request.


2. Go to the Authorization tab and select OAuth 2.0.


3. Click Get New Access Token and fill in:

Token Name: AzureB2CToken.

Grant Type: Authorization Code.

Auth URL: https://<tenant_name>.b2clogin.com/<tenant_name>.onmicrosoft.com/B2C_1_SignUpSignIn/oauth2/v2.0/authorize.

Access Token URL: https://<tenant_name>.b2clogin.com/<tenant_name>.onmicrosoft.com/B2C_1_SignUpSignIn/oauth2/v2.0/token.

Client ID: Application (Client) ID of PostmanClient.

Scope: Use the scope defined earlier (e.g., https://<tenant_name>.onmicrosoft.com/MyWebAPI/api.read).

Redirect URI: http://localhost.



4. Click Get Token. You’ll be redirected to the sign-in page.




---

b. Call the API

1. In Postman, create a new request.


2. Set the method (e.g., GET or POST) and API endpoint.


3. Under Authorization, select Bearer Token and paste the access token retrieved earlier.


4. Send the request.




---

Step 6: Validate and Debug

Use jwt.ms to decode and validate your access token.

Ensure the aud (audience) claim matches your API’s Application ID URI.

Check API logs for errors if the request fails.



